{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 3D Reconstruction from a Single Image using Depth Anything V2\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook demonstrates how to construct a **dense 3D point cloud** from a single 2D image. \n",
    "We use **Depth Anything V2** (via Hugging Face `transformers`) to predict pixel-wise depth maps, and then perform **inverse camera projection** (backprojection) using a virtual pinhole camera model to generate the 3D geometry."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Step 1: Import Dependencies & Check GPU Access"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import os\n",
    "import sys\n",
    "import urllib.request\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import torch\n",
    "from PIL import Image\n",
    "from transformers import pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "# Check CUDA availability for GPU acceleration\n",
    "device = 0 if torch.cuda.is_available() else -1\n",
    "print(f\"PyTorch version: {torch.__version__}\")\n",
    "print(f\"CUDA (GPU) Available: {torch.cuda.is_available()}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Using device: {'GPU' if device == 0 else 'CPU'}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Step 2: Download a Test Image\n",
    "We download a sample image (a dog sitting on grass) from the official PyTorch Hub repository."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "input_image_path = \"input.jpg\"\n",
    "sample_url = \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/pytorch/hub/master/images/dog.jpg\"\n",
    "\n",
    "if not os.path.exists(input_image_path):\n",
    "    print(f\"Downloading {sample_url}...\")\n",
    "    req = urllib.request.Request(\n",
    "        sample_url, \n",
    "        headers={'User-Agent': 'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64)'}\n",
    "    )\n",
    "    with urllib.request.urlopen(req) as response, open(input_image_path, 'wb') as f:\n",
    "        f.write(response.read())\n",
    "    print(\"Download completed successfully.\")\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    print(\"Test image already exists.\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Open and display image properties\n",
    "img = Image.open(input_image_path).convert(\"RGB\")\n",
    "print(f\"Image format: {img.format}, Size: {img.size}, Mode: {img.mode}\")\n",
    "img.resize((400, int(400 * img.height / img.width))) # Show resized version in notebook"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Step 3: Run Depth Estimation\n",
    "We load the `depth-anything/Depth-Anything-V2-Small-hf` model using Hugging Face's pipeline API."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"Loading Hugging Face depth-estimation pipeline...\")\n",
    "pipe = pipeline(task=\"depth-estimation\", model=\"depth-anything/Depth-Anything-V2-Small-hf\", device=device)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Running inference...\")\n",
    "result = pipe(img)\n",
    "\n",
    "# The model output contains raw depth map as PIL Image\n",
    "depth_img = result[\"depth\"]\n",
    "depth_img.resize((400, int(400 * depth_img.height / depth_img.width)))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Step 4: Mathematical Backprojection to 3D Camera Coordinates\n",
    "Under a standard pinhole camera projection, a 3D coordinate $(X, Y, Z)$ projects to a pixel $(u, v)$ as:\n",
    "$$u = \\frac{f_x \\cdot X}{Z} + c_x$$\n",
    "$$v = \\frac{f_y \\cdot Y}{Z} + c_y$$\n",
    "\n",
    "To backproject, we rearrange the formulas to compute $X$ and $Y$ for every pixel coordinate and its corresponding depth $Z$:\n",
    "$$X = \\frac{(u - c_x) \\cdot Z}{f_x}$$\n",
    "$$Y = \\frac{(v - c_y) \cdot Z}{f_y}$$\n",
    "$$Z = Z$$\n",
    "\n",
    "We'll define virtual intrinsic camera parameters:\n",
    "- Horizontal focal length $f_x$ & vertical focal length $f_y$ based on a 60-degree Field of View (FOV).\n",
    "- Principal point $(c_x, c_y)$ centered in the image.\n",
    "- Depth $Z$ is calculated from normalized disparity values (gray level) mapped onto a relative distance range (e.g., $1.0\\text{m}$ to $8.0\\text{m}$)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Convert images to numpy arrays\n",
    "color_arr = np.array(img)\n",
    "depth_arr = np.array(depth_img, dtype=np.float32)\n",
    "H, W = depth_arr.shape\n",
    "\n",
    "# 1. Define Intrinsics\n",
    "fx = fy = W * 0.866 # Approximate focal length for 60-degree FOV\n",
    "cx = W / 2.0\n",
    "cy = H / 2.0\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2. Convert raw disparity to Z\n",
    "disp_norm = depth_arr / 255.0\n",
    "min_z = 1.0\n",
    "max_z = 8.0\n",
    "Z = min_z + (1.0 - disp_norm) * (max_z - min_z)\n",
    "\n",
    "# 3. Grid Coordinates\n",
    "u, v = np.meshgrid(np.arange(W), np.arange(H))\n",
    "\n",
    "# 4. Apply Backprojection Equations\n",
    "X = (u - cx) * Z / fx\n",
    "Y = (v - cy) * Z / fy\n",
    "\n",
    "# 5. Flatten to list of vertices and colors\n",
    "points = np.stack((X, Y, Z), axis=-1).reshape(-1, 3)\n",
    "colors = color_arr.reshape(-1, 3)\n",
    "print(f\"Reconstructed {len(points)} 3D points successfully.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Step 5: Save as Stanford PLY File\n",
    "We save the point cloud as an ASCII `.ply` file with both geometry (x, y, z) and vertex colors (r, g, b)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "output_ply_path = \"output_pointcloud.ply\"\n",
    "print(f\"Saving point cloud to {output_ply_path}...\")\n",
    "\n",
    "header = (\n",
    "    \"ply\\n\"\n",
    "    \"format ascii 1.0\\n\"\n",
    "    f\"element vertex {len(points)}\\n\"\n",
    "    \"property float x\\n\"\n",
    "    \"property float y\\n\"\n",
    "    \"property float z\\n\"\n",
    "    \"property uchar red\\n\"\n",
    "    \"property uchar green\\n\"\n",
    "    \"property uchar blue\\n\"\n",
    "    \"end_header\\n\"\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "with open(output_ply_path, \"w\") as f:\n",
    "    f.write(header)\n",
    "    for i in range(len(points)):\n",
    "        f.write(\n",
    "            f\"{points[i, 0]:.4f} {points[i, 1]:.4f} {points[i, 2]:.4f} \"\n",
    "            f\"{int(colors[i, 0])} {int(colors[i, 1])} {int(colors[i, 2])}\\n\"\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"PLY Point Cloud saved successfully at: {os.path.abspath(output_ply_path)}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Step 6: Visualizing the Point Cloud\n",
    "You can load `output_pointcloud.ply` in standard free software to view it in 3D:\n",
    "1. **MeshLab** (Recommended): Select `File -> Import Mesh...` and navigate to the generated PLY file. Drag to rotate in 3D.\n",
    "2. **Blender**: File -> Import -> Stanford (.ply).\n",
    "3. **Online Viewer**: Drag and drop into an online viewer like [creators3d.com/online-viewer](https://www.creators3d.com/online-viewer)."
   ]
  }
 ],
